Graphify semantic refresh repair — 2026-09-27

Status: verified and published to graphify-out; limitations below remain explicit.
Baseline: commit 2def307; graph 1437 nodes / 2798 edges.
Reproduction: the rejected previous CLI extraction cache contains only 7 CHANGELOG
nodes versus 49 in the baseline, before build_merge. Several referenced files were
misattributed to their own paths rather than to the document that mentioned them.
The surviving cache also reuses generic ids across source files. The shrink guard
correctly identified incomplete extraction; disabling it is not a repair.

Repair scope: re-extract the six changed documents via Graphify's extraction skill,
reconcile prior entities against actual current text, rebuild changed code using
Graphify AST, then validate before replacing graph.json and its manifest/cache.
No runtime rules, existing memory entries, installer behavior or global tool versions
are changed. No forced shrink, HTML generation or full human report was performed.

Replacement gate: tools/graphify-check.py (read-only; Python standard library).
Invocation before Graphify cache/save/merge:
  python tools/graphify-check.py --root . --graph <previous-graph.json>
    --extraction <semantic.json> --review <reconciliation.json>
The reconciliation has a sources object keyed by repo-relative document path;
each entry contains source_sha256, retained/added ID arrays, and removed objects
with id and a source-grounded reason. The gate checks provenance, stable identity,
explicit removals, current bytes, ID collisions and endpoints; it cannot prove
that a model's removal reason or interpretation is correct. Inspect those against
the current source. AST-owned nodes are checked by the code extraction, not counted
as semantic document entities. New document nodes cannot claim AST ownership.

Executed proof:
- Windows and Linux: python -B qa/graphify-check-test.py -v, 21 tests, OK, exit 0.
- TDD: missing guard first failed an assertion; unscoped IDs and false AST ownership
  were accepted before their guards, then rejected with the guards in place.
- Isolated current test: pass / remove missing-entity check -> assertion FAIL /
  restore check -> pass. The public graph was never involved in that mutation.
- Saved rejected CLI cache: the new gate returned REJECTED/exit 1 before any merge.
- bash qa/lint.sh: PASS, core 9450/9500, contract 2998/3000. git diff --check: exit 0.
- Final AST preparation: 117 code files, failed_sources empty. Semantic cache save
  and reload: all six refreshed sources hit; every prepared semantic ID survives.

Second proven cause: Graphify 0.9.67 build_merge collapses document concepts whose
labels are bare code filenames, even with dedup=False. This discards documentary
identity and rationale; 13 old AST file nodes also incorrectly named README.en.md
as their source. New install.ps1 changed the canonical install.sh file-node ID.
tools/graphify-mentions.py now prepares the inputs: retain document IDs and context,
make their reference labels explicit, link to the unique actual code file, repair
AST provenance and rewire reviewed aliases. Ambiguous basenames are not guessed.
It writes a separate directory and refuses to overwrite it; inputs are unchanged.

Order for a future semantic refresh (the stock Graphify CLI is NOT patched):
1. Extract changed documents with the extraction skill and inspect reconciliation.
2. Run graphify-check.py against the prior graph, extraction and source-bound review.
3. Extract current AST with Graphify, then prepare document mentions:
   python tools/graphify-mentions.py --root . --graph <previous-graph.json>
     --ast <ast.json> --semantic <semantic.json> --out <new-prepared-directory>
4. Use the prepared graph and semantic data in Graphify build_merge with dedup=False;
   check retention of every unremoved old semantic ID and all fresh semantic IDs,
   source hashes and edge/hyperedge endpoints. Review edge removals separately.
5. Save/reload the prepared semantic cache and successful manifest in staging,
   verify there are no changed sources, then replace the public generated data.
Do not clear a shrink guard or accept an extraction solely because it is larger.

Actual-library negative control: the current build passed, then failed with the
same Graphify #479 preservation error when ONLY mention preparation was removed;
restoring that preparation passed. Nine unchanged-document identities would have
been lost without it. No public file was used for this mutation test.

Publication proof (after final test-file edit):
- 1771 nodes, 3597 edges, 60 hyperedges; 49 documents, 117 code files.
- SHA-256 matched current contents of all 166 detected sources after publication.
- Lost unremoved semantic IDs: 0; missing/dangling endpoints: 0; all hyperedges resolve.
- One obsolete HANDOFF report citation was explicitly replaced, not merged by name.
  Three obsolete/unsupported old edges were reviewed and removed; other-source edges
  and all nine reviewed prior document hyperedges were retained.
- detect_incremental: new_total=0, no deleted files.
- graph.json SHA-256: C340ACD03BDDDA6D2A8031B90ED836B3FA41CCF0EBF4EC05638F38C310754336.
- Before the final test-only AST refresh, the real Graphify extract CLI ran twice
  on staging with --no-cluster --max-workers 1, both exit 0, no document extraction.
  The second result was byte-identical; the first serialized the raw edge format.
  Read-only querying also worked on the published graph.
- Copy verification covered graph, manifest, analysis, labels and cache; no old
  caches or backups were deleted. The unrelated pre-existing backup stayed unchanged.

Limits: the standard detector does not parse 21 unclassified repository files.
Five JSON fixtures/configs have no AST nodes and stock CLI harmlessly queues them
again even with an up-to-date manifest; this did not trigger document/LLM work.
The AST extractor retains nine self-call edges (six already existed), including
incorrect super() resolution, and labelled external endpoints without local paths.
These are navigation limitations, not proven call semantics; read source to verify.
No general upstream AST repair is claimed. Existing HTML/GRAPH_REPORT and cost.json
were not refreshed; they remain historical. Subagent token usage is unavailable.
The graph is a navigation aid, never a substitute for current source evidence.
